display: drags that leave the window go on

In client mouse mode a drag stopped where the pointer left the widget: spice-gtk drops the window system's pointer
grab on every button press (so a drag can end on another display's widget), which stops the motion events from
outside the window; motion outside the widget was dropped anyway; and a release outside it too, so the guest kept
the button down. Moving a Windows window by its title bar in the desktop window stopped as soon as the pointer went
out of the window, and stuck to the pointer once it came back.

WinBoat shows one display per window: the grab stays, motion outside the widget with a button down goes to the guest
clamped to the display's edge, and releases always go to the guest.

--- a/src/spice-widget.c

--- a/src/spice-widget.c
+++ b/src/spice-widget.c
@@ -2234,6 +2234,14 @@
             y >= 0 && y < d->area.height) {
             spice_inputs_channel_position(d->inputs, x, y, get_display_id(display),
                                           button_mask_gdk_to_spice(motion->state));
+        } else if (motion->state & (GDK_BUTTON1_MASK | GDK_BUTTON2_MASK | GDK_BUTTON3_MASK)) {
+            /* Dragging past the widget's edge (a guest window by its title bar, a selection): the drag goes on
+             * along the display's edge instead of stopping where the pointer left */
+            spice_inputs_channel_position(d->inputs,
+                                          CLAMP(x, 0, MAX(d->area.width - 1, 0)),
+                                          CLAMP(y, 0, MAX(d->area.height - 1, 0)),
+                                          get_display_id(display),
+                                          button_mask_gdk_to_spice(motion->state));
         }
         break;
     case SPICE_MOUSE_MODE_SERVER:
@@ -2321,8 +2329,10 @@
     transform_input(display, button->x, button->y, &x, &y);
     if ((x < 0 || x >= d->area.width ||
          y < 0 || y >= d->area.height) &&
-        d->mouse_mode == SPICE_MOUSE_MODE_CLIENT) {
-        /* rule out clicks in outside region */
+        d->mouse_mode == SPICE_MOUSE_MODE_CLIENT &&
+        button->type != GDK_BUTTON_RELEASE) {
+        /* rule out clicks in outside region (but not the end of a drag that left it: the guest would keep the
+         * button down) */
         return true;
     }
 
@@ -2332,21 +2342,10 @@
             try_mouse_grab(display);
             return true;
         }
-    } else {
-        /* allow to drag and drop between windows/displays:
-
-           By default, X (and other window system) do a pointer grab
-           when you press a button, so that the release event is
-           received by the same window regardless of where the pointer
-           is. Here, we change that behaviour, so that you can press
-           and release in two differents displays. This is only
-           supported in client mouse mode.
-
-           FIXME: should be multiple widget grab, but how?
-           or should know the position of the other widgets?
-        */
-        ungrab_pointer(display);
     }
+    /* Client mode used to drop the window system's pointer grab here, so a drag could end on another display's
+       widget. WinBoat shows one display per window, and the grab is what brings the motion of a drag that leaves the
+       window (a guest window dragged by its title bar): it stays. */
 
     if (!d->inputs)
         return true;
